+   "file": "apps/desktop/src/metaproject/MetaprojectTree.tsx",
+   "problem": "Introduced by the fix for frontend-w1-F-001: the loading row and the Show more row of a parent share the key status:${parent}. If the Loading row has focus and the folder's first page arrives with a nextCursor, React reuses the keyed <li> and inserts up to 100 new entries before it, so focus stays on the same element, which is now 'Show more' after all the new children. The layout-effect restore does not run because the element is still connected. The last-page path (row removed) instead lands on the first child. The new tests only cover reads without a following nextCursor.",
+   "suggested_fix": "When a focused parent status row changes from loading to more, focus the first child row (remember the focused row's type with its index in the layout effect). Or give loading and more distinct ids again while keeping one id across error/loading and more/more-loading, so the index-based restore lands on the first child. Add a DOM test: focus a Loading row whose answer has nextCursor, release it, assert focus is on the first child. Operator decision: focus goes to the row now at the same place ('На то же место'); after a Loading row is replaced by children plus a Show more row, focus must land on the first child, consistent with the last-page path. Coordinate with r2-logic-w1-F-001 (anchor the hand-off to a surviving neighbour) and r2-performance-w2-F-001 (reuse the rows in memory instead of repeated querySelectorAll)."
+  },
+  {
+   "id": "r2-logic-w1-F-001",
+   "severity": "minor",
+   "file": "apps/desktop/src/metaproject/MetaprojectTree.tsx",
+   "problem": "This is new code from the fix for frontend-w1-F-001. When the focused row is removed, the layout effect focuses the element at `lost.index`. That index was recorded at the previous commit or focus event. If rows above the focused row are added or removed in the same commit that removes it, the index no longer points at the removed row's place. Focus then lands on an unrelated row, possibly in another folder.\n\nRepro: expand folders A and B (A above B) with both reads pending. Arrow onto B's Loading row. Both answers then arrive before React flushes, so one batched DefaultLane commit inserts A's children above and replaces B's Loading row. Focus goes to one of A's children at B's old index, not to B's first child. The same happens when a reveal or a Show more above commits in the same batch as the focused row's removal.",
+   "suggested_fix": "Anchor the hand-off to a surviving neighbour instead of a raw index. In `remember`, also record the `data-row` id of the row just before the focused one; for a status row that is its parent folder row `d:<parent>`. In the layout effect, if that row is still rendered, focus the element after it; otherwise fall back to the clamped index. Add a DOM test that holds two folder reads, focuses the lower folder's Loading row, releases both in one `act`, and expects focus on the lower folder's first child."
